readme.database -- PostgreSQL, Redis and worker threads
=======================================================

Services keep their data in PostgreSQL, with Redis in front of it, and
offer modules asynchronous access to both.  All of it is part of the
core: there is no database module to load.  The code is a port of ircu2's
database layer (include/db.h, include/cache.h, include/worker.h and the
drivers in modules/workers/ of ircu2), adapted to Services.

    include/store.h         the entity store: nicknames, channels, ...
    include/databases.h     the tables kept whole in memory (DBTable)
    include/db.h            database API for the core and modules
    include/cache.h         cache API
    include/worker.h        worker threads
    include/migration.h     the SQL that creates the tables
    src/store/              the entity store and its threads
    src/db.c, src/cache.c   calls in flight, the database and redis blocks
    src/worker.c            thread pool, dedicated workers, wake-up pipe
    src/postgres/           PostgreSQL driver, and the DBTable saves
    src/redis/              Redis driver and client
    src/migrations/         the core's own tables

The configuration is described in readme.config (blocks `database`,
`redis`, `workers`); the tables and how they are created, in
readme.migrations.

1. Where the data is
--------------------

### 1-1. The rule

    Services  =>  Redis  <=  PostgreSQL

PostgreSQL is the truth: the only place Services' data is kept for good.
Redis is the fast copy of it.  Services' memory holds only what is being
used right now.  However many nicknames and channels are registered,
Services hold in memory the records of the users who are online and of
the channels that exist, and nothing else; a network with five million
registered nicknames and eighty thousand users online has at most eighty
thousand nickname records in memory.  OperServ STATS ALL shows both
numbers: the records in the database, and how many of them are in memory.

### 1-2. The entity store

Nicknames, nickname groups (with their access lists, autojoin lists,
memos and memo ignore lists) and channels (with their access and autokick
lists) are kept by the entity store (include/store.h).  A module describes
each kind of record -- its tables, and how to turn the C structure into a
JSON "bundle" and back -- and then works with records as Services always
have: get one, use it, put it back.

READING.  A record is looked for in memory first (somebody is using it),
then among the writes not yet in the database, then in Redis, and only
then in PostgreSQL, whose answer is copied into Redis for next time.  "No
such record" is the commonest answer ("this nick is not registered"), and
it is cached too, for a shorter time (negative_ttl).

  - The paths that see the whole network go by -- a user connecting, a
    nick change, a channel being created, a netburst -- never wait for
    the answer.  The records are fetched in the background, in batches
    (one Redis MGET and one PostgreSQL query for up to 500 keys, however
    many users asked in the same pass of the main loop), and the user is
    checked when they arrive.  A burst of ten thousand users is a few
    dozen round trips.
  - A command that needs a record nobody is using (NickServ INFO on an
    offline nick, a REGISTER) asks synchronously, with a short deadline:
    `sync_timeout', one second by default.  That is the only time the
    main loop waits for the database.  A user who talks to Services
    before their background check has finished is checked on the spot.

MEMORY.  A record stays in memory while something holds it -- the user
using the nick, the channel that is registered, a timer -- and is freed
once the last holder lets go.

WRITING.  A record is written when it has changed: when it is let go for
the last time and, for records that stay in use (the nick of a user who
stays online), by a sweep that looks at a few of them every second and at
all of them every 30 seconds.  "Changed" means its bundle differs from the
one last written, so no code has to remember to say so.  The new bundle is
at once a pending write, which every read of that record is served from;
a writer thread then writes it to PostgreSQL, in transactions of many
records, in order, retrying until the database takes it, and after that
to Redis.  Only when both have it is the pending write dropped, so a
record never goes back in time, not even when Redis or PostgreSQL were
down for a while.

Services do not wait for any of this.  A change is in PostgreSQL within
seconds; at exit, Services wait (up to `save_timeout') for the writes
still on their way, and log how many could not be made.

### 1-3. The tables kept whole in memory

A few tables are small, and bounded by what operators do rather than by
the size of the network: OperServ's settings (oper), autokills (akill),
autokill exclusions (exclude), session exceptions (exception), S-lines
(sgline, sqline, szline), news, and StatServ's server statistics
(stat_servers).  These are still kept whole in memory, as a DBTable
(include/databases.h): loaded when their module is loaded, and saved
every `timeouts { update; }' (5 minutes by default), on OperServ UPDATE,
on SIGHUP and at exit.  A save snapshots every table in the main thread
and writes those that changed in a worker thread, in one transaction:

    BEGIN
    SELECT token FROM instance WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE
    TRUNCATE TABLE <table>; COPY <table> FROM STDIN      -- per table
    COMMIT

so it is atomic: either every changed table is written, or the previous
save stays in place, whole.  A save that fails is reported in the log and
by WALLOPS, and the next save tries again.  These tables are created and
extended by the save itself (a column a newer version adds is added), not
by migrations.

### 1-4. Layout

Every table lives in the configured schema ("services" by default), which
Services create if it is missing.

    migrations      every migration applied (readme.migrations)
    instance        which copy of Services owns the schema (1-5)
    store_stale     Redis keys to correct (1-6)
    nickgroups      nickname groups   nicks          nicknames
    nickgroup_access, nickgroup_ajoin, nickgroup_memos,
    nickgroup_memo_ignore             the lists of a group
    channels        channels          channel_access, channel_akicks
    oper, akill, exclude, exception, sgline, sqline, szline, news,
    stat_servers    the tables of 1-3

The first three are created by the core's migrations, the next by the
migrations of nickserv/main and chanserv/main
(modules/*/main/migrations/), the last by their modules' saves.  A table
of 1-3 is named after its DBTable, with anything other than a letter, a
digit or an underscore turned into an underscore; its columns are the
fields (memos.memomax becomes memos_memomax), plus `_seq', the row's
position:

    Field type          Column
    ----------          ------
    INT8, UINT8, INT16  smallint
    UINT16, INT32       integer
    UINT32              bigint
    TIME                bigint (seconds since the epoch)
    STRING              text (NULL for a NULL pointer)
    BUFFER              text (up to the first NUL)
    PASSWORD            bytea <name>, plus text <name>_cipher

The tables of the entity store follow the same conventions (times are
bigint seconds, passwords are bytea plus a _cipher column), and their keys
are the nick or channel name folded to lower case by IRC rules (nick_key,
name_key).

Strings are stored as UTF-8.  A string that is not valid UTF-8 (typically
old data in ISO-8859-1) is converted from ISO-8859-1 when it is written,
and the conversion is logged.

In Redis, a record is kept under <prefix>svc:<type>:<key>
(services:svc:nick:alice, services:svc:channel:#help), as its bundle: a
JSON object with the row of the main table under "main" and the rows of
each list under its own name.  "!" means "no such record".

### 1-5. Who owns the data

Services do.  They do not notice a row changed in the database by
something else, and the next write of that record overwrites it.  Read the
tables freely (a web panel, a report, a backup); write to them only while
Services are stopped, and then empty Redis too (or the keys you changed),
since it would otherwise go on serving the old copy until it expires.

Two copies of Services writing to one schema would overwrite each other in
turn, so a copy claims the schema when it starts: it takes an advisory
lock for as long as it runs (a second copy started while the first runs
refuses to start: "schema ... is in use by another copy of Services"),
and records a token of its own in the `instance' table.  Every write
checks that token inside its transaction, so a copy that has lost its
claim -- its connection dropped, and another copy started in the
meantime -- can no longer write.  Run with -readonly, Services read the
data but neither claim the schema nor write anything.

### 1-6. When PostgreSQL or Redis is not there

At start-up, Services need both: without PostgreSQL ("cannot connect to
the database") or Redis they do not start.  Running without their data
would let anybody register nicknames and channels that already belong to
someone.

While they run:

- REDIS RESTARTED.  An empty Redis costs queries until it fills again,
  and nothing else.  A Redis restarted from a snapshot is another matter:
  its copies may be older than the database, and would be served until
  they expire.  Run Redis without persistence (`save ""', `appendonly
  no'), or empty it whenever it restarts.

- REDIS DOWN.  Services carry on reading from PostgreSQL (a Redis that
  does not answer within `timeout' is skipped for a second before it is
  tried again).  Writes still go to PostgreSQL; a record written there
  but not to Redis has its key recorded in `store_stale', and when Redis
  answers again -- or when Services next start -- those keys are corrected
  or deleted, so that Redis never serves a copy older than the database.

- POSTGRESQL DOWN.  Records in memory and in Redis are served as usual,
  and changes are kept as pending writes (served to every read) and
  retried until the database takes them; the first writes after it comes
  back write everything that changed.  A record in neither place cannot
  be read: a user whose nick cannot be looked up is treated as using an
  unregistered nick (so is neither recognized nor warned), a command that
  needs such a record fails, and
  NOTHING IS CREATED whose absence cannot be confirmed -- a REGISTER of a
  nick or channel that might exist is refused ("registration failed")
  rather than risk replacing the real one when the database returns.
  The tables of 1-3 are not saved, with a WALLOPS each time.

- A module loaded later (REHASH) whose migrations cannot be applied, or
  whose DBTable cannot be loaded, does not load.  A DBTable that could not
  be loaded is never saved: saving it would replace the data in the
  database with an empty table.

### 1-7. Coming from the file databases

The database/standard (*.sdb files) and database/version4 (*.db files)
modules are gone, and so are the XML export and import modules that used
to carry data between versions.  Services do not read the old files: a
network moving to this version starts with an empty schema, which the
migrations create (readme.migrations), and registers its nicknames and
channels again.  PostgreSQL's own tools (pg_dump, pg_restore) are what
copies the data from then on.

2. Database access for modules (db.h)
-------------------------------------

### 2-1. The rules

A module that needs a database of its own -- tables beside the Services
tables, a query that does not fit in memory -- asks the core.  It does not
link against libpq, never sees a PGconn or a PGresult, and does not get a
thread of its own: it calls db_query(), and some time later its callback
runs in the main thread with the rows as JSON (jansson's json_t).

Three things are true of every query:

- A QUERY NEVER BLOCKS SERVICES.  db_query() hands the query to a pooled
  connection running in a thread of its own, and returns at once.
- A QUERY NEVER OUTLIVES FIVE SECONDS.  The timeout (`timeout`) is capped
  at five seconds, and enforced by the driver on its own clock: a
  connection to a machine that has stopped answering is cut off on time.
- A VALUE NEVER BECOMES SQL.  Statements are prepared and values are
  bound; there is no entry point that takes an assembled statement.  A
  parameter containing "'; drop table x; --" is a string that matches
  nothing.

### 2-2. Using it

    #include "db.h"

    static void score_loaded(const struct DbResult *res, void *user)
    {
        char *nick = user;
        User *u;

        if (res->err.dberr_code == DB_OK && (u = get_user(nick)) != NULL)
            notice(s_MyServ, u->nick, "Your score: %lld",
                   db_row_int(res->data, 0, "score"));
        free(nick);
    }

    ...
    struct DbParam  p      = { DB_TYPE_TEXT, u->nick, DB_FORMAT_TEXT };
    struct DbParam *args[] = { &p, NULL };
    struct DbQuery  q      = { "select score from scores where nick = $1",
                               args };
    char *nick = sstrdup(u->nick);

    if (db_query(THIS_MODULE, &q, score_loaded, nick) != DB_OK) {
        free(nick);
        notice(s_MyServ, u->nick, "Try again later.");
    }

db_exec() is the same call for statements that change data; it goes to
the `write` side of the configuration, db_query() to the `read` side.

Four rules for the caller, all about time passing:

- The query is copied, the result is not.  Everything in the DbQuery may
  be a local variable.  The DbResult is released when the callback
  returns: json_incref() its data to keep it.
- Do not keep a User * (or any pointer into Services' data) across a
  query.  The user may have quit by the time the answer arrives: keep the
  nick, and look it up again.
- Check the return value.  DB_OK means the callback will run; anything
  else is a refusal here and now, and the callback will never run.
- The callback may not run: if the module is unloaded first, its pending
  callbacks are dropped.  That is what lets a module be unloaded without
  waiting for the database.

Queries may be sent from init_module(), before Services have gone into the
background: they wait in the queue, and are run as soon as the connection
threads start.

### 2-3. What comes back

DbResult.data is a JSON array with one object per row, mapping column
names to values; a statement that returns no rows gets an empty array, and
DbResult.rows says how many rows it returned or touched.  Integers become
JSON integers; float4/float8/numeric, numbers; bool, true/false; json and
jsonb, the document itself; NULL, null; everything else (timestamps, uuid,
inet, arrays, bytea), a string.

A module that does not want to include jansson reads results with
db_rows(), db_row_str() and db_row_int().

Errors are a closed set (enum DbError): DB_ERR_UNIQUE, DB_ERR_FOREIGN_KEY,
DB_ERR_NOT_NULL, DB_ERR_RETRY (deadlock or serialization failure: run it
again), DB_ERR_TIMEOUT, DB_ERR_BUSY (every connection busy and the queue
full), DB_ERR_UNAVAILABLE, and a few more.  The message in
DbResult.err is the driver's own wording and safe to show to a user; the
database's own message, which names tables and columns, goes to the log
only.

### 2-4. One statement, and migrations

db_query() and db_exec() run one statement on one pooled connection;
there is no BEGIN/COMMIT across calls.  Work that must be atomic goes in a
function created by the module's migrations, called with one statement.

A module's tables are created by the migrations it ships in its
migrations/ directory, which an operator applies with OperServ MIGRATION;
see readme.migrations.  Every connection's search path is the configured
schema, then public, so a module's unqualified table names land beside
Services' own.

3. The cache (cache.h)
----------------------

A key-value store in front of the database, for a module that wants to
avoid asking the database the same question again and again.  It is the
same Redis the entity store uses (the `redis' block is required), under
the same prefix; the store's keys start with "svc:", which a module's
keys should not.  The first thing to understand: the cache is never the
truth.  Everything that reads it reads the database too when it misses; a
cache that is stale, slow or down costs a query and nothing else.  When
Redis cannot be reached, cache_get() returns 0, the callback never runs,
and the caller goes to the database as it would on a miss.

    cache_get(THIS_MODULE, "score:alice", got_score, data);
    cache_set(THIS_MODULE, "score:alice", json, len, 300, NULL, NULL);
    cache_del(THIS_MODULE, "score:alice", NULL, NULL);

The answer arrives in the main thread, as for queries.  The pattern is
read through (ask the cache; on a miss or any failure, ask the database
and write the answer back -- misses included), invalidate on write (after
writing to the database, cache_del() the keys that changed rather than
waiting for them to expire).

Values are binary-safe bytes (JSON is the natural choice); keys are at
most 255 bytes with the configured prefix, which the core adds; values at
most 64 KiB; expiries default to 5 minutes and are capped at a day.

4. Worker threads (worker.h)
----------------------------

The core stays single-threaded; the database and cache connections, the
store's writer and background fetches, the saves and the migrations run
in threads, and hand their results back to the main loop through a pipe
the socket layer watches.  A module may use
the same machinery for slow work of its own (worker_submit_owned() with
THIS_MODULE; a dedicated thread with worker_spawn_owned()).

The one rule: a worker thread never touches Services' state.  No User, no
Channel, no NickInfo, no smalloc()/free() from a file that includes
services.h (MEMCHECKS redefines them), no log() (use worker_log()), no
send_*().  Everything a worker needs is copied into its task; everything
it produces is copied out.  worker.h has the details.

Threads start once Services have forked into the background (a thread
does not survive a fork); work asked for before that is held until then.
Unloading a module drops its queued work and waits for any of its work a
thread is running.

5. Building
-----------

The core needs libpq, jansson and hiredis (see BUILDING.md):

    Debian, Ubuntu   apt install libpq-dev libjansson-dev libhiredis-dev
    Fedora           dnf install libpq-devel jansson-devel hiredis-devel
    Arch, Manjaro    pacman -S postgresql-libs jansson hiredis
    FreeBSD          pkg install postgresql16-client jansson hiredis

A module that includes <jansson.h> gets the include path from the build;
it links against nothing, like every module.
